course: dictionary
lesson: 5970
title: Matzah: a bread without yeast
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from a particular faith or culture, and the context around them will help you understand. «Passover, or Pesach, is a spring festival remembering the Exodus from Egypt, with the Seder meal and matzah, a flat bread made without yeast.» The sentence gives you the key clue: matzah is a flat bread made without yeast.
explain: Look at the other words too. Flat and bread tell you what kind of food matzah is. Without yeast is the most important clue, because yeast is what makes ordinary bread rise. Matzah stays thin and flat because it has none.
explain: The context also tells you why people eat it. Jewish people eat matzah at Passover to remember the Exodus, when the Israelites left Egypt in a hurry and had no time to let their bread rise. The story explains the bread, and the bread helps people remember the story.
fact: Matzah is a flat, thin, crispy bread made only from flour and water.
fact: Matzah is made without yeast, so it does not rise.
fact: Jewish people eat matzah during Passover to remember the Exodus from Egypt.
fact: Matzah is also called unleavened bread, which means bread without yeast.
fact: Matzah is eaten at the Seder meal, and it can be made into matzah ball soup.
exercise: What is matzah made without? | *Yeast | Flour | Water
exercise: Why do Jewish people eat matzah at Passover? | To celebrate the start of winter | To decorate the table | *To remember the Israelites leaving Egypt in a hurry, with no time for bread to rise | To make bread rise quickly
exercise: «Passover, or Pesach, is a spring festival remembering the Exodus from Egypt, with the Seder meal and matzah, a flat bread made without yeast.» Find the word that tells you what kind of bread matzah is. | Spring | *Flat | Pesach | Festival
test: Matzah is called unleavened bread. What does unleavened mean? | Sweet and sticky | Made with lots of yeast | *Made without yeast, so it does not rise | Cooked only on special days
test: Why is matzah linked to the Exodus? | It was invented in Egypt for the Exodus as a dessert | It is only eaten at birthday parties | It is a kind of sweet cake | *It is eaten at Passover to remember the escape from Egypt
exam: Jayden reads «matzah ball soup» and does not know what matzah is. What should he do? | Decide it must be a drink | Skip the whole text and never read it | Guess that matzah is a sweet | *Read the words around it, then check a dictionary if he is still unsure
